1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:06,900
Leonard, are you in the shower?

2
00:00:06,900 --> 00:00:10,040
I can't hear you! I'm in the shower!

3
00:00:10,040 --> 00:00:12,170
I asked if you were in the shower,

4
00:00:12,170 --> 00:00:14,140
but that's moot now!

5
00:00:14,140 --> 00:00:15,310
What?

6
00:00:15,310 --> 00:00:20,150
Moot! Rendered unimportant by recent events!

7
00:00:20,150 --> 00:00:22,720
I can't hear you! I'm in the shower!

8
00:00:22,720 --> 00:00:24,520
I have to skip the chitchat.

9
00:00:24,520 --> 00:00:25,790
Emergency.

10
00:00:25,790 --> 00:00:27,660
What kind of emergency?

11
00:00:27,660 --> 00:00:30,330
You get mathematical-- 32-ounce banana smoothie,

12
00:00:30,330 --> 00:00:32,360
16-ounce bladder.

13
00:00:32,360 --> 00:00:34,930
You might not want to do that.

14
00:00:34,930 --> 00:00:36,660
I assure you I do.

15
00:00:36,670 --> 00:00:39,270
Sheldon, I'm not alone in here.

16
00:00:39,270 --> 00:00:41,240
What?!

17
00:00:42,140 --> 00:00:44,070
Hello, Sheldon.

18
00:00:44,070 --> 00:00:45,540
What are you doing in there?

19
00:00:45,540 --> 00:00:46,810
She can't be in here!

20
00:00:46,810 --> 00:00:49,480
We were in here first; you can't be in here.

21
00:00:49,480 --> 00:00:51,150
According to the roommate agreement,

22
00:00:51,150 --> 00:00:52,650
paragraph nine, subsection B,

23
00:00:52,650 --> 00:00:54,380
"The right to bathroom privacy

24
00:00:54,380 --> 00:00:56,650
is suspended in the event of force majeure."

25
00:00:56,650 --> 00:00:58,320
And believe me, I am experiencing

26
00:00:58,320 --> 00:01:01,260
a very majeure force.

27
00:01:01,260 --> 00:01:03,760
Come on, you can't wait two minutes?

28
00:01:03,760 --> 00:01:06,690
Oh, Leonard, let the man pee.

29
00:01:11,430 --> 00:01:15,840
Penny? Penny? Penny?

30
00:01:15,840 --> 00:01:17,210
What?

31
00:01:17,210 --> 00:01:19,410
Move. Move. Move!

32
00:01:48,440 --> 00:01:50,680
Dr. Leonard Hofstadter,

33
00:01:50,680 --> 00:01:52,180
you are officially charged

34
00:01:52,180 --> 00:01:54,510
with two violations of the roommate agreement.

35
00:01:54,520 --> 00:01:57,780
Do you waive reading of the charges?

36
00:01:57,790 --> 00:01:58,920
Yeah, fine.

37
00:01:58,920 --> 00:02:00,250
Hang on. No.

38
00:02:00,260 --> 00:02:02,760
My client does not waive reading of the charges.

39
00:02:04,030 --> 00:02:07,030
Cool. I've got a lawyer.

40
00:02:07,760 --> 00:02:10,300
And I've seen her naked.

41
00:02:12,070 --> 00:02:13,630
Proceed.

42
00:02:13,640 --> 00:02:15,270
Very well.

43
00:02:16,140 --> 00:02:17,440
"Count the first:

44
00:02:17,440 --> 00:02:19,940
On or about the 28th day of April,

45
00:02:19,940 --> 00:02:22,740
the accused did knowingly and with malice aforethought

46
00:02:22,740 --> 00:02:26,240
deny access to the shared bathroom in a time of emergency,

47
00:02:26,240 --> 00:02:28,520
to wit, my back teeth were floating.

48
00:02:30,150 --> 00:02:32,750
Count the second: the accused exceeded

49
00:02:32,750 --> 00:02:35,060
the agreed upon occupancy of the shower,

50
00:02:35,060 --> 00:02:37,730
to wit, one, unless we are under attack

51
00:02:37,730 --> 00:02:39,590
by water-soluble aliens."

52
00:02:41,400 --> 00:02:43,760
Can I see the roommate agreement?

53
00:02:43,770 --> 00:02:46,300
Um, it's fairly technical.

54
00:02:47,670 --> 00:02:49,200
I think I can handle it.

55
00:02:50,140 --> 00:02:52,940
Top of her class, Cambridge University.

56
00:02:52,940 --> 00:02:55,340
Licensed to practice law in three countries

57
00:02:55,340 --> 00:02:57,110
and your face.

58
00:03:01,820 --> 00:03:04,820
All right, based on a cursory reading,

59
00:03:04,820 --> 00:03:07,520
it doesn't look like you have much of a case, Sheldon.

60
00:03:07,520 --> 00:03:10,420
Do so, do so.

61
00:03:10,430 --> 00:03:12,260
Oh, I'm afraid not.

62
00:03:12,260 --> 00:03:14,670
Section seven here, on the right to enter the bathroom

63
00:03:14,670 --> 00:03:17,460
in emergency situations, is not specific

64
00:03:17,470 --> 00:03:19,700
as to what constitutes an emergency.

65
00:03:19,700 --> 00:03:20,930
Oh, that's ridiculous!

66
00:03:20,940 --> 00:03:23,240
A bathroom emergency is self-explanatory.

67
00:03:23,240 --> 00:03:26,310
Is it? If Leonard forgot to trim his nose hair,

68
00:03:26,310 --> 00:03:28,710
could he barge in while you were showering?

69
00:03:28,710 --> 00:03:31,210
Irrelevant. Leonard doesn't trimis nose hair.

70
00:03:31,210 --> 00:03:34,820
He thinks because he's short nobody can see up there.

71
00:03:38,220 --> 00:03:40,750
My point is, Sheldon, the legal principle is,

72
00:03:40,760 --> 00:03:42,200
ambiguity in a contract benefits

73
00:03:42,200 --> 00:03:43,960
the party that did not draft it,

74
00:03:43,960 --> 00:03:45,230
in this case, Leonard.

75
00:03:45,230 --> 00:03:47,630
- So much for count one. - But...

76
00:03:47,630 --> 00:03:49,860
There's no buts, Sheldon; that's how the law works.

77
00:03:49,860 --> 00:03:51,600
Schooled!

78
00:03:56,140 --> 00:03:57,940
As for the shower capacity issue,

79
00:03:57,940 --> 00:03:59,770
I cite addendum J.

80
00:03:59,770 --> 00:04:02,280
"When Sheldon showers second, any and all measures

81
00:04:02,280 --> 00:04:05,280
shall be taken to ensure an adequate supply of hot water."

82
00:04:05,280 --> 00:04:08,380
I believe this supercedes the occupancy issue.

83
00:04:08,380 --> 00:04:11,450
Superceded!

84
00:04:14,990 --> 00:04:17,060
This isn't over.

85
00:04:19,760 --> 00:04:22,300
No offense, but shower sex with you

86
00:04:22,300 --> 00:04:24,800
is now the second best thing that's happened today.

87
00:04:27,570 --> 00:04:30,870
Mmm, Greek food on pizza night?

88
00:04:30,870 --> 00:04:33,330
This is the most delightfully cruel thing

89
00:04:33,330 --> 00:04:35,120
we've done to Sheldon since we left that fake message

90
00:04:35,120 --> 00:04:37,310
from Stephen Hawking on his voice mail.

91
00:04:37,310 --> 00:04:39,410
Seriously?

92
00:04:39,410 --> 00:04:42,880
I wish to discuss your theories of black holes.

93
00:04:44,450 --> 00:04:49,220
Meet me at the Randy's Donut by the airport at 2:00 a.m.

94
00:04:52,530 --> 00:04:54,530
What is that you're eating?

95
00:04:54,530 --> 00:04:56,300
Tonight is pizza night.

96
00:04:56,300 --> 00:04:59,530
I'd like to refer that to my attorney.

97
00:05:00,600 --> 00:05:02,500
According to what I see here,

98
00:05:02,500 --> 00:05:04,610
Thursday nights are Franconi's pizza night.

99
00:05:04,610 --> 00:05:07,140
Yes, and when Franconi's went out of business,

100
00:05:07,140 --> 00:05:09,210
we switched to Graziano's.

101
00:05:09,210 --> 00:05:10,510
That's interesting.

102
00:05:10,510 --> 00:05:14,280
Can you just switch restaurants like that, Priya?

103
00:05:15,720 --> 00:05:17,580
A good question, Howard.

104
00:05:18,450 --> 00:05:19,950
Turns out you can't.

105
00:05:19,950 --> 00:05:22,510
According to the document you drew up, Sheldon,

106
00:05:22,510 --> 00:05:24,710
the selection of a new takeout restaurant requires

107
00:05:24,710 --> 00:05:27,690
public hearings and a 60-day comment period.

108
00:05:27,700 --> 00:05:29,730
Were those criteria met?

109
00:05:36,170 --> 00:05:38,370
No.

110
00:05:39,070 --> 00:05:41,210
Opa! Opa! Opa!

111
00:05:42,710 --> 00:05:44,750
This is Greek food?

112
00:05:44,750 --> 00:05:46,810
Leonard, you hate Greek food.

113
00:05:46,810 --> 00:05:49,380
Not as much as you.

114
00:05:53,690 --> 00:05:57,220
Fine. I'm nothing if not adaptable.

115
00:05:58,030 --> 00:06:01,460
I got you the lamb kabob.

116
00:06:01,460 --> 00:06:03,500
Thank you.

117
00:06:23,790 --> 00:06:25,420
If you think about it,

118
00:06:25,420 --> 00:06:28,190
Greek food isn't that far from Italian food.

119
00:06:28,190 --> 00:06:30,690
They share a spice palette.

120
00:06:36,060 --> 00:06:39,570
And what a civilization is the Greeks'!

121
00:06:44,810 --> 00:06:48,540
They gave us science, democracy,

122
00:06:48,540 --> 00:06:52,450
and little cubes of charred meat that taste like sweat.

123
00:06:55,650 --> 00:06:59,490
Penny? Penny? Penny?

124
00:07:00,390 --> 00:07:01,620
Hey. What's up?

125
00:07:01,620 --> 00:07:05,530
The mean Indian lady tried to make me eat lamb.

126
00:07:07,730 --> 00:07:10,800
Congratulations. Pizza night will now be at your apartment.

127
00:07:10,800 --> 00:07:12,570
Order one.

128
00:07:12,570 --> 00:07:13,730
Oh, I'm sorry, honey,

129
00:07:13,730 --> 00:07:15,670
I'm meeting Amy and Bernadette for dinner.

130
00:07:15,670 --> 00:07:17,670
But you're welcome to tag along.

131
00:07:17,670 --> 00:07:18,940
A girls' night?

132
00:07:18,940 --> 00:07:20,600
Oh, I don't know if I'm up for an evening

133
00:07:20,600 --> 00:07:24,310
talking about rainbows, unicorns and menstrual cramps.

134
00:07:25,050 --> 00:07:26,680
Okay, suit yourself.

135
00:07:26,680 --> 00:07:29,220
We'll probably be trashing Priya a little.

136
00:07:29,220 --> 00:07:31,890
Shotgun.

137
00:07:32,620 --> 00:07:36,320
Okay, I'd like to propose a toast to a wonderful girls' night out.

138
00:07:37,130 --> 00:07:40,730
Fair warning-- we can get ca-razy.

139
00:07:40,730 --> 00:07:42,730
Yeah. Last week we smoked cigars

140
00:07:42,730 --> 00:07:44,800
and pretended to be dragons.

141
00:07:45,670 --> 00:07:47,430
Really, Amy?

142
00:07:47,440 --> 00:07:48,900
Tobacco and alcohol?

143
00:07:48,900 --> 00:07:52,040
Need I remind you not a lot of scientific discoveries were made

144
00:07:52,040 --> 00:07:54,610
by people having a good time?

145
00:07:57,220 --> 00:07:59,980
Why did you bring him? He's harshing my buzz.

146
00:08:00,880 --> 00:08:02,120
I felt sorry for him.

147
00:08:02,120 --> 00:08:04,150
Priya's giving him a hard time.

148
00:08:04,150 --> 00:08:07,660
Ooh, the Priya bashing's starting early. Yay!

149
00:08:08,560 --> 00:08:12,260
Okay, what's up with those pantsuits?

150
00:08:12,260 --> 00:08:14,090
I need some context.

151
00:08:14,100 --> 00:08:15,400
Pantsuits suck.

152
00:08:15,400 --> 00:08:16,830
And that opens her up

153
00:08:16,830 --> 00:08:18,870
to justifiable ridicule for wearing them.

154
00:08:18,870 --> 00:08:20,300
Good one, Bernadette.

155
00:08:21,200 --> 00:08:24,410
See? Ca-razy.

156
00:08:36,160 --> 00:08:38,230
You smell like baby powder.

157
00:08:39,300 --> 00:08:40,460
It's talc.

158
00:08:40,640 --> 00:08:42,900
But as that's a primary ingredient of baby powder,

159
00:08:42,900 --> 00:08:45,370
I understand your confusion.

160
00:08:45,370 --> 00:08:48,940
Oh, I'm not confused at all.

161
00:08:48,940 --> 00:08:52,140
You're like a sexy toddler.

162
00:08:53,650 --> 00:08:55,980
I don't know how to process that.

163
00:08:56,980 --> 00:08:58,350
I do.

164
00:08:59,350 --> 00:09:02,450
Amy and Sheldon sitting in a tree.

165
00:09:02,450 --> 00:09:06,360
K-I-S-S-S-S-I-N-G.

166
00:09:07,170 --> 00:09:09,490
That's too many S's for "kissing."

167
00:09:09,490 --> 00:09:12,760
Not if they're doing it for a long time.

168
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:17,200
Sheldon... have you ever kissed a girl?

169
00:09:17,200 --> 00:09:21,420
Other than my mother, my sister and my Mee-Maw, no.

170
00:09:21,420 --> 00:09:23,320
But in the interest of full disclosure,

171
00:09:23,320 --> 00:09:24,790
I was once on a bus

172
00:09:24,790 --> 00:09:27,870
and had to give mouth-to-mouth resuscitation to an elderly nun

173
00:09:27,870 --> 00:09:30,210
who passed out from heat exhaustion.

174
00:09:31,180 --> 00:09:33,250
Every year I get a Christmas card from her,

175
00:09:33,250 --> 00:09:36,790
signed with far too many X's and O's.

176
00:09:36,790 --> 00:09:38,660
That doesn't count.

177
00:09:38,660 --> 00:09:40,830
Aren't you even a little curious?

178
00:09:40,830 --> 00:09:42,490
Yeah, you're a scientist--

179
00:09:42,490 --> 00:09:44,700
where is the curiosity?

180
00:09:44,700 --> 00:09:47,270
I'm available for experimentation.

181
00:09:50,770 --> 00:09:52,570
Thank you.

182
00:09:52,570 --> 00:09:53,810
Not necessary.

183
00:09:53,810 --> 00:09:56,070
We know everything there is to know about kissing.

184
00:09:56,080 --> 00:09:58,030
It requires 34 facial muscles

185
00:09:58,030 --> 00:09:59,940
and 112 postural muscles.

186
00:09:59,950 --> 00:10:01,110
Oh, blah, blah, blah.

187
00:10:01,110 --> 00:10:03,180
Give me some sugar, bestie.

188
00:10:06,910 --> 00:10:07,980
I'm certainly glad no one said

189
00:10:07,980 --> 00:10:11,820
they were curious about Aztec human sacrifice.

190
00:10:11,820 --> 00:10:14,690
Hey, I know-- let's take Sheldon dancing.

191
00:10:14,690 --> 00:10:16,930
Ooh, I totally want to see Sheldon dance.

192
00:10:16,930 --> 00:10:20,130
I bet he looks like a spider on a hot plate.

193
00:10:21,230 --> 00:10:23,370
No, thank you, but for the record,

194
00:10:23,370 --> 00:10:24,600
I'm an excellent dancer.

195
00:10:24,600 --> 00:10:27,310
Proficient in the Rumba, Waltz and Cha-cha.

196
00:10:27,310 --> 00:10:28,410
Really?

197
00:10:28,410 --> 00:10:30,070
I don't see why that's surprising.

198
00:10:30,080 --> 00:10:31,810
I excel at so many things.

199
00:10:31,810 --> 00:10:34,310
You've had my sourdough bread.

200
00:10:34,310 --> 00:10:36,780
Did you take dance lessons?

201
00:10:36,780 --> 00:10:38,150
Against my will,

202
00:10:38,150 --> 00:10:40,280
in the South, preadolescent children

203
00:10:40,290 --> 00:10:42,720
are forced through a process called cotillion,

204
00:10:42,720 --> 00:10:45,090
which indoctrinates them with all the social graces

205
00:10:45,090 --> 00:10:49,930
and dance skills needed to function in 18th century Vienna.

206
00:10:49,930 --> 00:10:54,030
Oh, we are so taking you dancing.

207
00:10:54,030 --> 00:10:56,400
No, you most certainly are not.

208
00:10:56,400 --> 00:10:59,070
Well, what does your cotillion training say is expected

209
00:10:59,070 --> 00:11:01,270
of a gentleman when three ladies ask him

210
00:11:01,270 --> 00:11:03,310
to escort them to a dance soiree?

211
00:11:06,380 --> 00:11:08,650
I saved a nun's life,

212
00:11:08,650 --> 00:11:10,880
Why am I being punished?

213
00:11:17,860 --> 00:11:19,790
Take us someplace we can waltz.

214
00:11:19,790 --> 00:11:21,030
Where you can what?

215
00:11:21,030 --> 00:11:23,760
Waltz. It's a social dance from Austria

216
00:11:23,760 --> 00:11:26,800
choreographed to a three-four time signature.

217
00:11:40,180 --> 00:11:43,050
Oh, waltz.

218
00:11:46,350 --> 00:11:48,190
Here we go.

219
00:11:50,460 --> 00:11:54,330
*I kissed a girl and I liked it*

220
00:11:54,330 --> 00:11:56,560
*I hope my boyfriend don't mind...*

221
00:11:56,560 --> 00:11:58,430
What happened to you?

222
00:12:18,050 --> 00:12:20,650
Shake it baby, shake it!

223
00:12:20,650 --> 00:12:23,550
Muy caliente, Sheldon!

224
00:12:23,560 --> 00:12:25,590
Care to dance?

225
00:12:25,590 --> 00:12:28,430
Oh, I'm sorry, I'm engaged.

226
00:12:28,430 --> 00:12:30,430
How about you?

227
00:12:30,430 --> 00:12:33,700
Oh, what the hell.

228
00:12:52,280 --> 00:12:54,590
How come if we're the smart people,

229
00:12:54,590 --> 00:12:56,920
we don't do this every night?

230
00:12:56,920 --> 00:12:59,520
What's 16 times 14?

231
00:12:59,530 --> 00:13:03,060
My burps taste like cranberry juice.

232
00:13:04,860 --> 00:13:07,530
And there's your answer.

233
00:13:07,530 --> 00:13:10,430
Would you like to come in for a nightcap?

234
00:13:10,440 --> 00:13:12,270
If you're referring to the beverage,

235
00:13:12,270 --> 00:13:13,810
you know I don't drink.

236
00:13:13,810 --> 00:13:15,310
If you're referring to the hat you don

237
00:13:15,310 --> 00:13:19,610
while wearing a nightshirt and holding a candle, I have one.

238
00:13:20,680 --> 00:13:21,980
I have Yoo-hoo.

239
00:13:21,980 --> 00:13:24,480
It's hard to say no to Yoo-hoo.

240
00:13:25,580 --> 00:13:27,750
The name literally beckons.

241
00:13:31,560 --> 00:13:33,020
Make yourself comfortable.

242
00:13:33,030 --> 00:13:34,260
Thank you.

243
00:13:35,830 --> 00:13:37,660
Is someone smoking?

244
00:13:37,660 --> 00:13:39,600
Oh, that's just Ricky.

245
00:13:44,700 --> 00:13:46,700
You own a smoking monkey?

246
00:13:46,710 --> 00:13:48,670
Don't be silly. He's one of the animals

247
00:13:48,670 --> 00:13:50,810
in my department's nicotine addiction study.

248
00:13:52,540 --> 00:13:54,310
What's he doing here?

249
00:13:54,310 --> 00:13:55,610
I'm giving him emphysema.

250
00:13:55,610 --> 00:13:58,780
The least I can do is let him hang out and watch Cable.

251
00:13:59,620 --> 00:14:02,050
Remarkable.

252
00:14:03,090 --> 00:14:05,120
Aren't you worried about secondhand smoke?

253
00:14:05,120 --> 00:14:07,490
A little. The real danger is him biting

254
00:14:07,490 --> 00:14:09,290
my face off while I'm sleeping.

255
00:14:12,630 --> 00:14:15,700
Is he deliberately blowing smoke at me?

256
00:14:15,700 --> 00:14:18,770
Yeah. He's kind of an ass.

257
00:14:22,210 --> 00:14:23,670
Thank you.

258
00:14:24,740 --> 00:14:26,480
May I share something with you

259
00:14:26,480 --> 00:14:27,950
that's troubling me?

260
00:14:27,950 --> 00:14:28,950
Of course.

261
00:14:28,950 --> 00:14:31,450
What's rattling around that big bulbous head of yours?

262
00:14:31,450 --> 00:14:34,290
Priya has essentially nullified

263
00:14:34,290 --> 00:14:36,220
my roommate agreement with Leonard,

264
00:14:36,220 --> 00:14:39,990
making life in the apartment very uncomfortable for me.

265
00:14:39,990 --> 00:14:43,330
And you want me to kill her? Done.

266
00:14:43,330 --> 00:14:45,200
No, of course not.

267
00:14:45,200 --> 00:14:46,200
I trained Ricky how to smoke.

268
00:14:46,200 --> 00:14:47,670
I can train him to shoot a poison dart.

269
00:14:47,670 --> 00:14:51,000
No jury would convict us 'cause people love monkeys.

270
00:14:52,370 --> 00:14:53,840
I understand

271
00:14:53,840 --> 00:14:56,070
the alcohol has stirred up whatever it is

272
00:14:56,070 --> 00:14:58,140
that makes girls go wild...

273
00:14:58,140 --> 00:15:02,110
but I really need to talk to smart Amy now.

274
00:15:03,280 --> 00:15:05,180
Excuse me.

275
00:15:06,120 --> 00:15:07,820
Have you considered that your intelligence

276
00:15:07,820 --> 00:15:12,090
might be the very thing causing your dilemma?

277
00:15:16,900 --> 00:15:18,060
No.

278
00:15:18,060 --> 00:15:19,360
What do you think Ricky over here would do

279
00:15:19,360 --> 00:15:22,000
if an interloper encroached on his territory?

280
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:26,870
Well, when challenged, monkeys generally assert their dominance

281
00:15:26,870 --> 00:15:32,240
through chasing, assault and a stylized penile display.

282
00:15:32,240 --> 00:15:34,680
That's a little outside my comfort zone.

283
00:15:34,680 --> 00:15:36,180
You're being too literal.

284
00:15:36,180 --> 00:15:39,520
My point is, he would not meekly surrender to the rules,

285
00:15:39,520 --> 00:15:41,250
and neither should you.

286
00:15:41,250 --> 00:15:44,190
Are you suggesting I play dirty?

287
00:15:44,190 --> 00:15:46,520
Yes, dirty.

288
00:15:46,530 --> 00:15:50,960
Dirty... dirty... dirty.

289
00:15:53,470 --> 00:15:56,230
Which brings me to our next order of business.

290
00:16:00,110 --> 00:16:02,840
Fascinating.

291
00:16:02,840 --> 00:16:05,080
I hope you don't take what I'm about to do

292
00:16:05,080 --> 00:16:07,110
as a comment on what we just did.

293
00:16:21,360 --> 00:16:24,700
Who's to say you shouldn't be dissecting our brains?

294
00:16:26,430 --> 00:16:28,830
You really are an ass.

295
00:16:31,940 --> 00:16:33,770
Morning, Sheldon.

296
00:16:33,770 --> 00:16:35,540
Oh, good, you're up.

297
00:16:35,540 --> 00:16:37,640
I've written a new and improved

298
00:16:37,640 --> 00:16:40,340
roommate agreement that benefits me greatly.

299
00:16:40,350 --> 00:16:42,380
I'd like you to sign it.

300
00:16:43,180 --> 00:16:45,250
Why would I want to do that?

301
00:16:45,250 --> 00:16:47,020
Excellent question.

302
00:16:47,020 --> 00:16:49,950
Do you remember what happened to the alien,

303
00:16:49,960 --> 00:16:52,720
played by talented character actor Frank Gorshin,

304
00:16:52,720 --> 00:16:56,860
in the Star Trek episode "Let That Be Your Last Battlefield"?

305
00:16:56,860 --> 00:17:00,770
Uh, Captain Kirk activated the self-destruct sequence

306
00:17:00,770 --> 00:17:03,000
and threatened to blow up the Enterprise

307
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:05,240
and kill them both unless he gave in?

308
00:17:05,240 --> 00:17:06,500
Affirmative.

309
00:17:06,510 --> 00:17:10,540
Computer, this is Dr. Sheldon Cooper.

310
00:17:12,010 --> 00:17:14,050
Activate self-destruct sequence.

311
00:17:14,050 --> 00:17:16,650
Code 1-1-A-2-B.

312
00:17:16,650 --> 00:17:19,320
Self-destruct sequence activated.

313
00:17:23,220 --> 00:17:25,360
What are you gonna do, Sheldon,

314
00:17:25,360 --> 00:17:26,760
blow up the apartment?

315
00:17:26,760 --> 00:17:31,200
That was my first thought, but all my cool stuff is here.

316
00:17:31,200 --> 00:17:33,760
So what happens when it counts down?

317
00:17:33,770 --> 00:17:36,000
Unless Leonard signs the new agreement

318
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:38,070
in the next 41 seconds,

319
00:17:38,070 --> 00:17:42,010
this computer will send an e-mail to your parents in India

320
00:17:42,010 --> 00:17:44,240
saying that you're in a secret relationship

321
00:17:44,240 --> 00:17:46,610
with the whiter-than-Marshmallow-Fluff

322
00:17:46,610 --> 00:17:48,280
Leonard Hofstadter.

323
00:17:48,280 --> 00:17:51,120
Oh, my God.

324
00:17:51,120 --> 00:17:52,220
What's the big deal?

325
00:17:52,220 --> 00:17:53,780
Trust me, it's a big deal.

326
00:17:53,790 --> 00:17:55,890
They're gonna find out about me eventually, right?

327
00:17:55,890 --> 00:17:57,390
Yeah, of course, just not today.

328
00:17:57,390 --> 00:17:58,960
20 seconds.

329
00:17:58,960 --> 00:18:00,120
Are you ashamed of me?

330
00:18:00,130 --> 00:18:01,330
Of course not.

331
00:18:01,330 --> 00:18:02,960
Then why can't we tell your parents?

332
00:18:02,960 --> 00:18:04,300
Please, don't push this.

333
00:18:04,300 --> 00:18:06,560
He does that all the time, doesn't he? 15.

334
00:18:06,570 --> 00:18:08,600
Okay, fine, Sheldon, you win. Turn it off.

335
00:18:08,600 --> 00:18:09,670
No, he's bluffing.

336
00:18:09,670 --> 00:18:12,000
- I never bluff. Ten. - It's blackmail!

337
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:13,070
- Nine. - We give up.

338
00:18:13,070 --> 00:18:15,110
- Eight. - This is ridiculous.

339
00:18:15,110 --> 00:18:16,870
It's a laptop with a full charge.

340
00:18:16,880 --> 00:18:18,410
Honestly, what do you see in him?

341
00:18:18,410 --> 00:18:20,340
Give him what he wants or we're done.

342
00:18:20,350 --> 00:18:21,310
- Three. - Really?

343
00:18:21,310 --> 00:18:22,880
- Two. - Okay, I'll sign it!

344
00:18:22,880 --> 00:18:27,550
Self-destruct sequence aborted.

345
00:18:30,990 --> 00:18:32,720
You may have gone to Cambridge,

346
00:18:32,720 --> 00:18:35,790
but I'm an honorary graduate of Starfleet Academy.

347
00:18:48,870 --> 00:18:51,040
Good morning, Amy.

348
00:18:53,410 --> 00:18:57,150
It most assuredly is not.

349
00:18:57,150 --> 00:19:00,580
Are you experiencing dehydration, headache,

350
00:19:00,580 --> 00:19:03,750
nausea and shame?

351
00:19:03,750 --> 00:19:05,620
Yes.

352
00:19:05,620 --> 00:19:07,890
I also found a Korean man's business card

353
00:19:07,890 --> 00:19:10,330
tucked into my cleavage.

354
00:19:10,330 --> 00:19:12,590
What happened last night?

355
00:19:12,600 --> 00:19:14,500
Ah, memory impairment;

356
00:19:14,500 --> 00:19:19,100
the free prize at the bottom of every vodka bottle.

357
00:19:19,100 --> 00:19:20,240
Sheldon?

358
00:19:20,240 --> 00:19:21,400
All right.

359
00:19:21,400 --> 00:19:24,970
Last night you gave me some excellent advice

360
00:19:24,970 --> 00:19:26,340
regarding my problem here at home,

361
00:19:26,340 --> 00:19:30,780
you kissed me and then vomited on and off for 40 minutes,

362
00:19:30,780 --> 00:19:34,580
following which you passed out on your bathroom floor.

363
00:19:34,580 --> 00:19:37,690
I then folded a towel under your head as a pillow,

364
00:19:37,690 --> 00:19:39,620
set your oven clock to the correct time

365
00:19:39,620 --> 00:19:41,790
because it was driving me crazy...

366
00:19:42,960 --> 00:19:44,590
...and I left.

367
00:19:44,590 --> 00:19:47,760
Okay.

368
00:19:47,760 --> 00:19:51,330
Don't really know where we go from here.

369
00:19:52,170 --> 00:19:54,000
I suggest we treat our relationship

370
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:56,200
as if it were a crashed computer

371
00:19:56,210 --> 00:19:59,770
and restore it to the last point we both agree it worked.

372
00:19:59,780 --> 00:20:02,140
Terrific. Thank you.

373
00:20:03,710 --> 00:20:05,450
They were out of menthols!

374
00:20:05,450 --> 00:20:07,250
Get off my back!

375
00:20:10,450 --> 00:20:13,590
Not easy living with a temperamental little primate.

376
00:20:13,590 --> 00:20:16,020
Come on, Priya, just admit I embarrass you!

377
00:20:16,030 --> 00:20:18,130
You're preaching to the choir, sister.

